@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_PORT_VERSION@-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Share > Receive opens LocalSend to receive files, and Install >
    Service > Chromium Account lets Chromium sign in to a Google account.
  * The ic, ix and icx aliases open tdl's tmux layout with OpenCode,
    Claude Code or both.
  * After the first update, a one-time notification offers to install
    dictation with Voxtype.
  * Install > Service > 1Password also installs its Chromium extension;
    the removal takes the extension out.
  * Fingerprint authentication skips the reader while the laptop lid is
    closed: Setup > Security > Fingerprint enables the new pam-auth-update
    profile @DISTRO_ID@-fprintd, Ubuntu's fprintd profile with a pam_exec
    gate in front. Run the setup again to switch an existing setup over.
    The package's prerm removes both of its PAM profiles.
  * Setup > DNS also sets NetworkManager's global DNS, so networks joined
    later use the chosen servers. A polkit rule
    (50-@DISTRO_ID@-global-dns.rules) lets the users who may change network
    connections change it without a password, as for the connections.
  * Hardware fixes for other machines, applied at install on a device
    match: the xe options for ASUS ExpertBook B9406 and Zenbook UX5406AA
    displays, Intel Quick Sync and i965 video drivers, and b43 firmware
    for the Broadcom BCM4331.
  * The inotify watch limit is 524288 again (90-@DISTRO_ID@-file-watchers.conf),
    over localsearch's 65536.
  * whois is preinstalled.
  * The Wi-Fi 7 fix (iwlwifi disable_11be) applies to Dell XPS laptops on
    Panther Lake only; the postinst removes it from other machines with an
    Intel BE200 or BE211, which get Wi-Fi 7 back at the next boot.
  * The maintainer scripts call programs by name (Debian Policy 6.1), and
    lintian overrides with reasons cover the sample hooks, the copied
    first-run hooks, the shell's sanitizer strings, the migration runner's
    dpkg lock wait and the commands without manual pages.
  * The pacman sample hook (pre-refresh-pacman.d) is gone: Ubuntu has no
    pacman and the port has no "refresh pacman".

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 21:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (4.0.4+git20261016.53.d0a9b09a-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * The firewall is on: apply-system allows LocalSend's port (53317 tcp
    and udp) and turns ufw on for the next boot, on Ubuntu's policies
    (deny incoming), without Omarchy's Docker rules. Systems installed
    earlier keep ufw off.
  * Install and Remove > Development no longer offer PHP or Laravel.
  * Wi-Fi power save is off again (wifi.powersave = 2 in
    /etc/NetworkManager/conf.d/@DISTRO_ID@-wifi-powersave.conf): power save
    causes latency spikes and link drops on Intel BE200/BE211 firmware.
  * btop, starship, eza, zoxide, lazygit and inxi are preinstalled again,
    with Omarchy's btop, lazygit and starship configurations, btop's theme
    sync and the Activity binding (Super+Ctrl+T); a migration seeds the
    configurations for existing users. Depend on ttfx, so the screensaver
    runs. The clipboard's image action and imv's Ctrl+E open swappy.
  * Pause Hyprland's automatic config reload while @DISTRO_ID@-config is
    unpacked (new preinst) and resume it with one reload in the postinst.
  * systemd-oomd may kill the user's apps (app.slice) but no longer the
    compositor: Ubuntu's kill policy on user@.service is set back to auto.
  * SSH keepalive for every host (ServerAliveInterval 15), so the shell's
    ssh wrapper notices a dropped connection.
  * unzip, GNOME Disks and GNOME Calculator are preinstalled; the
    calculator takes Super+Ctrl+Q and the Calculator key. Drop
    @DISTRO_ID@-desktop-apps, whose only application moved here.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261016.45.f82dbf50-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Setup > Security > TPM Unlock: opt-in unlock of the encrypted root disk
    by the TPM at boot through Clevis, bound to PCR 7 (Secure Boot state);
    Remove > Security > TPM Unlock takes it off. Depend on clevis-luks,
    clevis-tpm2 and clevis-initramfs.
  * tmux gives extended keys to every xterm-type terminal, so Shift+Enter
    reaches programs such as Claude Code inside tmux in Foot, Alacritty and
    Ghostty; a migration fixes the line in existing users' tmux.conf.
  * @DISTRO_ID@-update takes a Timeshift snapshot of / before updating on
    btrfs installs and keeps the newest five of its own (new
    @DISTRO_ID@-snapshot; restore opens Timeshift).
  * The launcher starts applications whose desktop id ends in .desktop,
    such as Telegram's org.telegram.desktop.
  * The fork's tests are sorted for the port (the fork's PORT.md, Tests).
  * Omarchy's agent skills are shipped again, ported to Ubuntu, and linked
    for Claude Code, Codex, Pi and Hermes (a migration links them for
    existing users); their bug reports go to the fork, santisbon/omarchy,
    whose name the rename step now keeps (DISTRO_FORK_REPO).

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Fri, 16 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261015.39.7a4a96b4-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * LibreOffice's launchers are named Writer, Calc, Impress, Draw, Base and
    Math: the snap names them "LibreOffice <version> Writer" and so on, which
    the launcher cut off. Install > Productivity > LibreOffice writes
    per-user copies with the short names (new
    @DISTRO_ID@-libreoffice-snap-launchers), Remove deletes them, and a
    migration writes them for a snap installed before.
  * Depend on quickshell (>= 0.3.2-0@DISTRO_ID@2), whose lock screen works
    more than once per login.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 15:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261015.38.846f58d7-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Install > Productivity > LibreOffice installs the Snap Store's
    libreoffice (Canonical) instead of Flathub's, whose build drew its
    toolbar icons nearly invisible on the dark theme and its Save dialog
    larger than the screen; the launcher hides the snap's Start Center.
  * Menu > System > Suspend shows "Suspending" on screen first, as Reboot
    and Shutdown show their messages (new @DISTRO_ID@-system-suspend).
  * About (fastfetch): the OS line reads /etc/os-release ("Oaxaca 26.10")
    instead of the desktop package's version.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 14:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261015.35.c94677d3-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Natural scrolling on touchpads (Hyprland's input.touchpad.natural_scroll);
    mouse wheels keep the classic direction.
  * Screenshots: swappy's Ctrl+S saves the annotated image next to the
    screenshot as screenshot-<date>-edited.png and closes swappy; the
    screenshot stays as captured. It had saved a second copy in the home
    folder and written the drawing over the screenshot on close. New users
    get ~/.config/swappy/config; a migration copies it for existing ones.
  * Removing a Flatpak app no longer fails with "failed to execute child
    process dbus-launch", and now deletes the app's data: root uninstalls
    the app, the user deletes ~/.var/app/<id> and resets its permissions.
  * The notification after a shell restart (the Dictation installer's
    "Voxtype Dictation Ready") no longer fails with "Call failed: The name
    is not activatable": the restart waits for the notification service.
  * Setup > Defaults > Editor lists nano; Learn > Ubuntu, Thunderbird's
    entries and Install > Development > mise have icons again.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Thu, 15 Oct 2026 12:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261014.30.bca7233b-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * New Productivity category: Install > Productivity > LibreOffice
    installs LibreOffice from Flathub (org.libreoffice.LibreOffice,
    published by The Document Foundation); Remove > Productivity >
    LibreOffice removes it. The app launcher hides the
    Start Center and XSLT filter entries (the Flatpak's and the archive
    package's) and now shows Base, Draw and Math, which Omarchy hides.
  * tldr preinstalled again, as Ubuntu's tealdeer package (a Rust client
    that installs the same tldr command as Omarchy's Python client), with
    ~/.config/tealdeer/config.toml turning on auto_update so the pages
    download on first use; a migration copies that file for existing users.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 22:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261014.28.963b196e-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * tmux preinstalled again, with Omarchy's configuration, theme sync,
    Super+Alt+Return (the Work session), Super+Alt+K (its keybindings) and
    the Learn and Update > Config menu entries.
  * nano preinstalled as the default editor: the editor fallback named it,
    and the image had no terminal editor at all.
  * Neovim preinstalled again, without Omarchy's LazyVim configuration;
    Learn > Neovim opens Neovim's quick reference.
  * HEY and Basecamp removed: the HEY key bindings (Super+Shift+C, E,
    Alt+E) and HEY as the email link handler, which named a launcher the
    port never shipped; a migration drops that handler from existing users.
  * Super+Shift+E opens Thunderbird, bound only while its snap is
    installed; the Thunderbird install and remove entries reload Hyprland.
  * Papers preinstalled as the document viewer and the PDF default, in
    place of Evince, which only the optional desktop-apps package had;
    desktop-apps keeps GNOME Disks.
  * swappy preinstalled as the screenshot editor that the screenshot
    notification opens, in place of Omarchy's Tensaku, which has no Ubuntu
    build; Ctrl+S saves the annotations over the screenshot.
  * Install > AI > Dictation and Remove > Dictation are back: Voxtype from
    this distro's repository, with GPU mode set per user on the discrete
    GPU, isolated between dictations. The bar's Dictation indicator opens
    the installer while Voxtype is missing.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 19:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261014.26.87da7b10-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Depend on rfkill: the bar's Bluetooth switch lifts the radio's rfkill
    block with it, and Ubuntu ships the command in its own package, so the
    switch could not turn Bluetooth on.
  * The launcher rescans after a Flatpak install or removal: it watched only
    the application folders that existed when the shell started, so the
    first Flatpak app did not appear.
  * The Snap picker asks for its search term with bash's prompt (gum's drew
    twice on the first window resize) and wraps the classic-confinement
    explanation.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 18:00:00 -0500

@DISTRO_ID@-desktop (@OMARCHY_VERSION@+git20261014.25.92b6de53-0@DISTRO_ID@1) @BASE_SERIES@; urgency=medium

  * Install > Package and Remove > Package show Ubuntu's logo (Nerd Font
    linux-ubuntu) instead of Arch's (md-arch): the entries install from
    Ubuntu's archive.
  * Install > Snap and Install > Flatpak pick apps from the Snap Store and
    Flathub in fzf, as Install > Package does from Ubuntu's archive; the
    Snap picker searches first and asks before installing a classic snap.
    Remove > Snap and Remove > Flatpak list the installed apps.

 -- @MAINTAINER@  Wed, 14 Oct 2026 13:00:00 -0500

